/// An iOS device registered for Apple Push Notification service.
///
/// ─── Why this is not PushSubscription ───────────────────────────────
///
/// `PushSubscription` is Web Push and nothing else: an `endpoint` URL that is
/// unique because it IS the address, plus `p256dh` and `auth` — the browser's
/// keys, used to encrypt the payload so the push service cannot read it.
///
/// APNs shares none of that. There is no endpoint (the host is Apple's, and
/// differs by environment), no per-device encryption keys (the connection is
/// authenticated to Apple with a JWT signed by a .p8 key), and the failure
/// semantics differ — a `BadDeviceToken` or `Unregistered` from Apple is
/// permanent in a way a Web Push 500 is not.
///
/// Folding the two into one table would mean a discriminator, roughly half the
/// columns inapplicable per row, and `endpoint @unique` — the thing that stops
/// a browser accumulating duplicate rows — no longer being the right key.
///
/// ─── environment is load-bearing ────────────────────────────────────
///
/// SANDBOX and PRODUCTION are DIFFERENT APNs hosts, and a token minted for one
/// is rejected by the other. Getting this wrong produces the classic
/// "notifications work in TestFlight and not in the App Store" — which looks
/// like a certificate problem and is not. It is stored per device because the
/// same user can have a debug build and a released one on two phones.
model DeviceToken {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  userId String

  /// The APNs device token, hex. Rotates — iOS reissues it on reinstall, on
  /// restore from backup, and occasionally for its own reasons, so the client
  /// re-registers on every launch and this row is upserted.
  deviceToken String

  /// Which app this token belongs to. An APNs push carries it as `apns-topic`,
  /// and a token is only valid for the bundle it was issued to.
  bundleId String

  environment ApnsEnvironment @default(PRODUCTION)

  /// Cosmetic, for a "your devices" screen: "iPhone 15 Pro", "iOS 18.2".
  deviceName String?
  osVersion  String?

  createdAt     DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt     DateTime  @updatedAt
  lastSuccessAt DateTime?

  /// Consecutive failures. Apple telling us a token is dead removes the row
  /// outright; this counts the transient failures that have not yet resolved.
  failureCount Int @default(0)

  /// One row per token per environment. The same hex string CAN legitimately
  /// exist in sandbox and production — they are different namespaces — so the
  /// environment is part of the key. Without it, a developer's debug build
  /// would evict their own production registration.
  @@unique([deviceToken, environment])
  @@index([userId])
  @@map("device_token")
}
